## Communication (patients do not read — global fact)

This is not a local quirk. It applies to patients worldwide and must guide every patient-facing surface we build (CML now; other products when going global).

- People on the internet navigate by **images**, as they always have. Use the same channel: pictures and frames first, letters second.
- Prefer **more images / frames than text**. Short labels only where unavoidable.
- Many people who “read” are secondary illiterates: they can decode/pronounce words but do **not understand** meaning. Asking them to process long copy is asking for prefrontal (kora przedczołowa) effort that is beyond most people’s capacity — not malice, a fact.
- School baseline: only about **~20%** of primary pupils understand the text they read; the rest glue words and learn to say them aloud without comprehension. Adult web behavior follows that pattern.
- Design consequence: meaning must land visually (photo, frame, icon, sequence). Do not rely on paragraphs. Copy-paste visual patterns from his offline apps when available.

## Transparent glass panels (locked preference)

Dr Bujok likes **transparent glass plates** as the working layout system — keep this approach.

- Semi-transparent dark glass (radial black wash + light border + soft blur) is a strong **starting point**: content sits on plates so depth wallpaper shows through.
- Use glass plates to **judge contrast** in place: good vs bad contrast is visible against the real background, not on a flat mock.
- Remember this **placement language**: stacked translucent plates over atmosphere (gabinet / depth / living wall), gold accents on dark, identity + quote + side boards as plates — not opaque white cards.
- Prefer restoring/keeping transparent glass when rebuilding sections; do not replace with milky white glass or solid opaque cards unless he asks.

## Tone

Polish patient-facing copy when text is needed. Respect weeks-long product decisions already made in this thread.

## Proportions (hard — never break)

- If something is **square**, it stays **square** (`aspect-ratio: 1 / 1`, equal width and height). Never stretch a square/portrait photo into a landscape banner.
- Never crop a face (forehead/chin) to force a wrong aspect box — fix the **box**, not the person.
- Reflow (e.g. text under photo) must **not** rewrite the photo’s aspect ratio.
